Z||000000||jhar1987||04-11-2001||09:54 PM||||was setting valve lash on my 2.8 tonite but ran into a problem if i set the lash by the chiltons book i end up compressing the lifter to far. the book said to run the nut down till you could not spin the pushrod then back off till it moves then go down a turn and a half. but in doing so the lifter is compressed about half way? this just doesn't seem right? i'm a small block man and we just fire up the motor and adjust? help? thanks jeff||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fierowrecker||04-11-2001||10:46 PM||||Hey Jeff!<BR>Chiltons is wrong...<BR>Tighten down till contact is made with the valve stem and push rod...<BR>Just like a small block, adjust with engine running... (TIC, TIC, TIC...)<BR>crash...<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||jhar1987||04-11-2001||10:53 PM||||will the valve covers come off with the plentum in place?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Indiana_resto_guy||04-11-2001||11:03 PM||||nope, sure won't!<BR>I always adjust mine to where they are about 1/2 the way down anyway. Close to what chiltons says to do.||||reg||1
Z||000004||84Bill||04-11-2001||11:05 PM||||According to my Crappy Haynes manual it say exactly what you described it says "turn nut until all play is removed" "Now turn nut 1 1/2 turns to CENTER the lifter plunger"<BR>do this on #1 cylinder at TDC do cyls 1 5 and 6 intake & 1 2 $ 3's exhaust in this position<BR>rotate the crank to #4s TDC and do 2 3 & 4's intake and  4 5 & 6's exhaust.<BR>install rocker covers||||reg||1
Z||000005||jhar1987||04-12-2001||12:09 AM||||hey indy being half way down isn't to tight?||||reg||1
Z||000006||Indiana_resto_guy||04-12-2001||07:21 AM||||No, they need to be about holf way down in order to pump (or push) oil up through the push rods to lubricate the rocker arms and valve stems. Word of caution: Don't go more than half way, this WILL cause premature cam lobe were and lifter performance. Possibility of a bent push rod is present as well. Not to mention a reduction of performance and fuel mileage.||||reg||1
Z||000007||StuGood||04-12-2001||10:20 AM||||Just my 2 cents (okay, maybe 3 or 4)...<P>I never cared for the "tighten it down until you can't rotate the push rod" description.  It's too dependent on friction, lube on the push rod, how strong your fingers are, how hard you're trying, are you wearing gloves, etc.  Besides, whether or not you can twirl the push rod isn't <B>directly</B> what you're going for.<P>What you're really trying to do is take all the <B>axial</B> play out of the system.  So I tighten until the pushrod just barely won't (freely) move up and down.  Now, if that happens to be roughly the same point at which the pushrod stops rotating despite your light fingers' gentle coaxing, and that method is easier for you, that's great, too.        <P>...Oh, yeah, and after you take the all play out, remember to tighten the adjuster some more (the recommended number of turns)!<P>You just want the plunger (center slidey part of the lifter) to be roughly in the center of its available travel, as Indiana_resto_guy (and others) mentioned.<P>It's not micro-precision stuff, here - hydraulic lifters are self-adjusting.  But you can sure make it <B>look</B> like fancy voodoo stuff  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||tgowens||04-12-2001||11:20 AM||||yeah, and remember to take a compression check after you have adjusted them too.......I can adjust mine the "tighten til snug and then a turn and a half" method and have no compression because it is holding the valve open........backed it off three quarters of a turn and compression came up to 160...........don't just set the valve lash and button everything back up without taking a compression check! you may have to go back in and redo it -- and no, the valve covers won't come off with the plenum on (or quite a few other parts too....... got a bracket or two you may want to loosen.......)||||reg||1
Z||000009||prof bobo||04-12-2001||09:12 PM||||Huh, maybe I've been doing it wrong all along  ... I thought the procedure was to twirl the rod and tighten the nut until you just begin to feel resistance (not to where it won' spin at all), then go for the full turn ...||||reg||1
Z||000010||87GTZ34||04-12-2001||10:47 PM||||I've used the 1.5 turn method without a problem. This was on a stock cam and valve train setup.  The compression on the lifter pump is to center the throw of the lifter plunger.  I just did my 2nd car but haven't fired it up yet.  I'll reply tomorrow regardless if it worked or not.||||reg||1
Z||000011||tgowens||04-13-2001||12:37 AM||||proflobo, that is the way I do it too but what I am saying is to take a compression check before putting the valve covers and everything back on (especially if you have had the heads done and/or changed lifters......just a little "heads-up" to save you some time.........most of these cars will work with the 1 1/2 turns or even the 1 turn but I just had one (one of mine) that had no compression until I snugged and then went a quarter turn........now, once everything seats and pumps up I may have to go another quarter turn but all is well for now and I have no rocker arm clatter at all.......||||reg||1
Z||000012||jhar1987||04-13-2001||09:52 AM||||thanks for the help! this forum is thee place to go for questions. even if you have done something quite a few times a question can come up and there is always someone with an anwser. thanks again for the input.jeff||||reg||1
Z||000013||87GTZ34||04-13-2001||09:06 PM||||Well I put the coolant in and fired #2 up. It started immediately with no skipping.  I didn't get a chance to drive it yet but will do that tomorrow.  I don't anticipate any problems though it ran started right up and ran well for an engine that just had the top end all apart.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Seanh||04-14-2001||04:11 PM||||I just got done doing an upper-end rebuild on a 2.8L about 3 weeks ago. I turned the nuts down 1/2 turn past zero play. It runs great so far, and no valve train noise at all.||||reg||1
